{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":1796213,"reputation":3431,"user_id":21986,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/991a4a6ddb04ff0a0d0ca93849f9b0ac?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"learner101","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/21986/learner101"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1602603170,"post_id":40649,"comment_id":85168,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"He&#39;s 9 months old now, though. Should the ramp have happened by now? How can I tell?"},{"owner":{"account_id":19654101,"reputation":165,"user_id":39681,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Gjspf9y5c5bb6o2BCtnq7eN9zXezor-aKZFAZAJ=k-s256","display_name":"Joseph Massaro","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39681/joseph-massaro"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1796213,"reputation":3431,"user_id":21986,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/991a4a6ddb04ff0a0d0ca93849f9b0ac?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"learner101","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/21986/learner101"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1602604813,"post_id":40649,"comment_id":85171,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"There&#39;s a handy checklist here: <a href=\"https://www.nidcd.nih.gov/health/speech-and-language\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">nidcd.nih.gov/health/speech-and-language</a>  Just make sure he&#39;s coming along with the things in the 7mo to 1yr section.  Around 1 yr when the explosion happens, his change in vocabulary and speech complexity will be easy to notice."},{"owner":{"account_id":3319177,"reputation":221,"user_id":29958,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/dTTna.jpg?s=256","display_name":"scipper","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/29958/scipper"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1603453317,"post_id":40678,"comment_id":85288,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thank you, for the answer. Very interesting, that there are studies about this. But a little bit disappointing, that there are no meaningful differences."},{"owner":{"account_id":5182392,"reputation":445,"user_id":22177,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/55DUG.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Amazon Dies In Darkness","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/22177/amazon-dies-in-darkness"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1605576694,"post_id":40772,"comment_id":85514,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I think your statement &quot;...for small children, the effects of screen time are overwhelmingly negative.&quot; can likely be applied to people of all ages, including adults. :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":1312213,"reputation":4224,"user_id":9368,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2442ea911c89187262d6575d739a1ca7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"swbarnes2","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9368/swbarnes2"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1605584856,"post_id":40772,"comment_id":85516,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"But older kids and grown-ups at least can learn from passively watching screens.  Babies can&#39;t."},{"owner":{"account_id":1859446,"reputation":291,"user_id":9412,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/28421dffa7471c8852849161418fb842?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Tracy Cramer","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9412/tracy-cramer"},"edited":false,"score":5,"creation_date":1605595375,"post_id":40775,"comment_id":85519,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;d add music to the list."},{"owner":{"account_id":3932364,"reputation":3398,"user_id":17525,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/EFWrn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Adam Heeg","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/17525/adam-heeg"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605611381,"post_id":40779,"comment_id":85524,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I was thinking this as well, plus you may want to add anecdotal research about the negative impact on children of parents who push or expect too much."},{"owner":{"account_id":189496,"reputation":215,"user_id":25566,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/J477b.png?s=256","display_name":"TonyK","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/25566/tonyk"},"edited":false,"score":9,"creation_date":1605613978,"post_id":40775,"comment_id":85526,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Note to OP: <i>mobile</i> means <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_(sculpture)\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this</a>, not <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/CrPAvN29Nhs\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this</a>!"},{"owner":{"account_id":5706210,"reputation":101,"user_id":32641,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f6f574617919d7bcf84b9c30df85f28d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"DreamConspiracy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32641/dreamconspiracy"},"edited":false,"score":7,"creation_date":1605617411,"post_id":40779,"comment_id":85531,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Can you cite a source for this answer? The child in question is 6 months old, and to my knowledge there is no IQ test for children of this age, and as such it would be impossible to confirm that &quot;your child&#39;s IQ is what it is.&quot; Furthermore, <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/a/40773/32641\">this answer</a> seems to cite a study in which certain types of parent behavior did have a significant positive effect on a number of factors that look a lot like IQ (and likely IQ as well)."},{"owner":{"account_id":5706210,"reputation":101,"user_id":32641,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f6f574617919d7bcf84b9c30df85f28d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"DreamConspiracy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32641/dreamconspiracy"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1605617820,"post_id":40784,"comment_id":85532,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The first sentence in this answer directly disagrees with the opinion of a strong majority of psychologists- the overwhelming opinion is that general intelligence exists, and IQ measures it well. See <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G_factor_(psychometrics)\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">the associated Wikipedia page</a> for the full details."},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"anon_241C427E5F7241D3A70ED4967"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":5706210,"reputation":101,"user_id":32641,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f6f574617919d7bcf84b9c30df85f28d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"DreamConspiracy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32641/dreamconspiracy"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605618350,"post_id":40784,"comment_id":85534,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;IQ tests do not measure intelligence but rather a correlate with a weak causal link to intelligence&quot; -  Have the DOI if you want to look up the paper 10.1037//0033-2909.101.2.171"},{"owner":{"account_id":5706210,"reputation":101,"user_id":32641,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f6f574617919d7bcf84b9c30df85f28d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"DreamConspiracy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32641/dreamconspiracy"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605619137,"post_id":40784,"comment_id":85535,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The sentence you quoted starts with &quot;The hypothesis that best fits the results is that.&quot; That means the paper you are quoting has 1) not done a scientifically rigorous study to determine that the statement you made is true, and 2) decided to try and fit the evidence to a hypothesis <i>in the abstract of the paper,</i> something which the scientific method requires you <b>not</b> to do. So yes, I stand by the scientifically rigorous claims made in the metastudy/survey that wikipedia cites as opposed to the untested hypothesis of one author."},{"owner":{"account_id":2460796,"reputation":101,"user_id":35520,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/01472229eb35acfd71dfd90d094c16bc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Manuki","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/35520/manuki"},"edited":false,"score":23,"creation_date":1605622260,"post_id":40775,"comment_id":85537,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I would add NATURE. Get the child to know and spend time in nature and natural things. The sun light, the rain, bodies of water, plants, gardening, observing animals and insects. Definitely avoid swiping and scrolling machines and time-eating TV &quot;just to keep the kid quiet&quot;"},{"owner":{"account_id":1554324,"reputation":357,"user_id":27621,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a7c19e1d6860e0e908fe3bdd165f3eb9?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"RonJohn","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27621/ronjohn"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":5706210,"reputation":101,"user_id":32641,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f6f574617919d7bcf84b9c30df85f28d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"DreamConspiracy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32641/dreamconspiracy"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605624056,"post_id":40779,"comment_id":85539,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@DreamConspiracy that answer also quoted an article which said that the children of &quot;talkers&quot; were <b>1.5x higher</b> than the children of taciturn parents, and that&#39;s patently impossible (a child having an IQ of 250 -- or &quot;only&quot; 150&quot;, depending on how you interpret &quot;1.5x higher&quot; -- instead of 100 is absurd)."},{"owner":{"account_id":4604845,"reputation":141,"user_id":33664,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/104f87d3a3e1def1c4f8e45c28687d55?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Kvothe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33664/kvothe"},"edited":false,"score":13,"creation_date":1605624274,"post_id":40777,"comment_id":85541,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"You claim to know a lot of things a lot better than other people. Can you please back this up with some actual sources. A lot of the claims seem blatantly false to me. For example I think current scientific consensus does not agree with &quot;You can&#39;t make a child more clever. That&#39;s built in, by nature.&quot; Development during children&#39;s youth affect their adult IQ. (See for example <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4511162/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4511162</a> but an expert can probably give you a much better reference.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":3795627,"reputation":2433,"user_id":7440,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/ulVTa.png?s=256","display_name":"Peter - Reinstate Monica","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7440/peter-reinstate-monica"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1605627603,"post_id":40778,"comment_id":85543,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"This. There is &quot;too much focus on academics&quot; not only in the early years. Academic achievement is not equivalent to intelligence, and intelligence is not equivalent to a well-developed and content personality. The OP&#39;s child will likely be on the intelligent side no matter what. But will it be happy?"},{"owner":{"account_id":309598,"reputation":101,"user_id":14054,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/4f23cb856b0d68fcc9fbd41c5d59f215?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Davor","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14054/davor"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1605630010,"post_id":40780,"comment_id":85544,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"5-6 <i>meters</i> from the screen? That can&#39;t be right, you would need an absolutely enormous room to sit that far, and you&#39;d need something like a 100&quot; (250cm) screen at the minimum to actually watch from that distance."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1605632182,"post_id":40782,"comment_id":85546,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"You&#39;ve got a lot of specific claims in there; your answer would be improved by a source or two, at least.  And as far as Algebra &quot;not done in the regular curriculum&quot;, the Common Core has Algebraic Thinking concepts introduced in first grade (6); it&#39;s true that I&#39;d support doing more at that stage, but they definitely are setting the stage for formal Algebra earlier these days (and getting a TON of pushback from parents who don&#39;t understand it!)"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605635589,"post_id":40779,"comment_id":85558,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"What&#39;s your source for this belief/answer? IQ tests are fallible; they do not truly measure a person&#39;s &quot;intelligence&quot;. They are influenced by culture (plagued by bias in those making up the test, e.g. white kids did better than black kids because some of the questions were about things that only white kids were likely to have encountered), motivation (kids do better on the same test some days than on others), coaching, etc. One of many sources supporting this comment: <a href=\"https://www.discovermagazine.com/mind/do-iq-tests-actually-measure-intelligence\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">discovermagazine.com/mind/&hellip;</a>."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1605636461,"post_id":40779,"comment_id":85562,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"-1. This is an unsupported bias expressed as an answer. It really should be downvoted and flagged."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1605637068,"post_id":40775,"comment_id":85563,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;...take advantage of every minute you have with her.&quot; That sounds absolutely exhausting when stated that way. It sounds like hyperuberOCD parenting. Kids are work, yes, but they aren&#39;t little machines to be programmed. The more fun you have with kids (mutual fun), the more you&#39;ll enjoy interacting with them. Also, babytalk is <i>important</i>. Lecturing a kid on something that  interests you but has noting it it for the baby will do nothing for enrichment. Babytalk emphasizes vowel/common consonant sounds, is sing-songy, melodic, repetitive, and accompanied by interesting facial expressions..."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1605637400,"post_id":40775,"comment_id":85565,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"...while a lecture on fluid dynamics/gardening/genetics/neuroscience/medicine/medieval history/Latin/etymology/photography (all of which I find interesting) would just be a waste of breath and time baby-wise, <b>unless</b> I did it all in babytalk! <a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0963721415595345\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">(Baby)Talk to Me: The Social Context of Infant-Directed Speech and Its Effects on Early Language Acquisition</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":522706,"reputation":279,"user_id":18949,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/bffff1c42112f47e7cbcde48a6e55e92?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"JVC","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/18949/jvc"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3795627,"reputation":2433,"user_id":7440,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/ulVTa.png?s=256","display_name":"Peter - Reinstate Monica","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7440/peter-reinstate-monica"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1605640978,"post_id":40778,"comment_id":85568,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"As a bit of an addition, I&#39;m also horrified at the trend of not allowing children to read or learn things on their own if they are deemed &quot;too young for that&quot;.  If a child has an interest in something, no matter how complex or advanced you may think it is, <i>let them have at it!</i>  I was reading at 2 and read anything and everything I could get my hands on.  I had to gradually learn over many years to dial it wayyyyy down so I could survive in the (American) public school system."},{"owner":{"account_id":232345,"reputation":126,"user_id":14821,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5aedd802dca0d3b7397664dfac645ae8?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"anonymous","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14821/anonymous"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605642779,"post_id":40785,"comment_id":85572,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"On the other hand, there is the well-documented successful case of the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%A1szl%C3%B3_Polg%C3%A1r#Education_and_career\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Polg&#225;r sisters</a>."},{"owner":{"account_id":6115116,"reputation":2985,"user_id":14637,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/pDGmO.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Graham","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14637/graham"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":4604845,"reputation":141,"user_id":33664,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/104f87d3a3e1def1c4f8e45c28687d55?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Kvothe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33664/kvothe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605643325,"post_id":40777,"comment_id":85574,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Kvothe IQ is tricky anyway, because not everyone (Stephen Gould, famously) believes that it is a good indicator of &quot;intelligence&quot;.  Early IQ tests can be a pointer to academic success, true - but that is strongly confounded by quality of teaching and parenting, and by the fact that taking an IQ test is a skill in its own right.  Again, we&#39;re in the realms of teaching a child skills, not &quot;making them more clever&quot;."},{"owner":{"account_id":6115116,"reputation":2985,"user_id":14637,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/pDGmO.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Graham","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14637/graham"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":4604845,"reputation":141,"user_id":33664,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/104f87d3a3e1def1c4f8e45c28687d55?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Kvothe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33664/kvothe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605643560,"post_id":40777,"comment_id":85575,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Kvothe As for knowing this &quot;a lot better than other people&quot;, every book on parenting, and every parenting class, will tell you every point I&#39;ve mentioned above.  I&#39;d like to say &quot;literally every one&quot;, but to be a bit more correct I&#39;m going to say 100% with several sigmas error."},{"owner":{"account_id":5962695,"reputation":183,"user_id":27841,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/cDBWJ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"henning no longer feeds AI","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27841/henning-no-longer-feeds-ai"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1605645248,"post_id":40777,"comment_id":85577,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The &quot;lastly&quot; should come first, and that&#39;s about the only fault I can find in this excellent answer."},{"owner":{"account_id":5962695,"reputation":183,"user_id":27841,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/cDBWJ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"henning no longer feeds AI","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27841/henning-no-longer-feeds-ai"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1605645836,"post_id":40775,"comment_id":85579,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anongoodnurse could it be that babytalk also does more for the baby than a monologue on gardening etc. is because it means there is actual <i>connection</i>?"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":5962695,"reputation":183,"user_id":27841,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/cDBWJ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"henning no longer feeds AI","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27841/henning-no-longer-feeds-ai"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1605646374,"post_id":40775,"comment_id":85580,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@henning--reinstateMonica - Sure, not only possible, but probable! (Also, thanks for pointing that out.) Even so, at <i>6 months</i>, I suspect asking, &quot;Do sweet peas grow on trees? I don&#39;t think so!!!&quot; would have about the same effect as, &quot;Does my honey-bunny have a wet diaper? Let&#39;s see!!!&quot; As the child refines their ability to make connections, the latter becomes more important, while the former becomes whimsical."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605647546,"post_id":40775,"comment_id":85583,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anongoodnurse I don&#39;t think it&#39;s intended to be exhausting, and I don&#39;t think anything in my answer suggests an unreasonable focus on &quot;IQ&quot;.  Take advantage of every minute is not intended literally. :)  And as for baby talk vs. not baby talk - as I said, &quot;if you&#39;re not one for baby talk&quot;.  Any talking is better than not talking!"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605648299,"post_id":40775,"comment_id":85585,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe - &quot;Any talking is better than not talking!&quot; Is it really? I don&#39;t share that view; would it be distinctly better than, say, playing a high quality recording for the baby while you tote them around? I also suspected you didn&#39;t mean &quot;take advantage of every minute&quot; literally, but it still sounds prohibitively exhausting! (I didn&#39;t accuse you of being unreasonably focused on IQ. I said it sounded exhausting.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":6569274,"reputation":1412,"user_id":17787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jTz7e.png?s=256","display_name":"chasly - supports Monica","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/17787/chasly-supports-monica"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605650836,"post_id":40782,"comment_id":85589,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Children don&#39;t learn languages by having private tutors, they learn by assimilation."},{"owner":{"account_id":160089,"reputation":2002,"user_id":9837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7ee099fe1b5382177ba5645dbb1d50bc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"R.. GitHub STOP HELPING ICE","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9837/r-github-stop-helping-ice"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605665527,"post_id":40774,"comment_id":85608,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The claim in the first bullet point is clearly false. It might be true at under 3 or under 6 months, but 10-12 month babies who can <i>walk</i> can absolutely equate movement/dancing on a screen to doing the same things with their own bodies."},{"owner":{"account_id":160089,"reputation":2002,"user_id":9837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7ee099fe1b5382177ba5645dbb1d50bc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"R.. GitHub STOP HELPING ICE","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9837/r-github-stop-helping-ice"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605665615,"post_id":40788,"comment_id":85609,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"My first child absolutely learned to count from Barney at under 18 months. The idea that this is not possible is absurd."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":160089,"reputation":2002,"user_id":9837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7ee099fe1b5382177ba5645dbb1d50bc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"R.. GitHub STOP HELPING ICE","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9837/r-github-stop-helping-ice"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605669367,"post_id":40774,"comment_id":85610,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"You’re welcome to present evidence of that; the link in my post, as well as others I’ve read, support the lack of connection between screen and real life. I would caution that imitating the screen doesn’t mean they understand the equivalence; and of course every baby is different."},{"owner":{"account_id":1554324,"reputation":357,"user_id":27621,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a7c19e1d6860e0e908fe3bdd165f3eb9?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"RonJohn","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27621/ronjohn"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1605669849,"post_id":40779,"comment_id":85611,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"MODERATORS: very few answers have citations, and <b>only one</b> of the answers (<a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/a/40790/27621\">parenting.stackexchange.com/a/40790/27621</a>) has a citation to a scholarly article, and it&#39;s strongly skeptical of a focus on praising intelligence).  Why is my answer being picked on?"},{"owner":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605671674,"post_id":40778,"comment_id":85612,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@JVC &quot;I&#39;m also horrified at the trend of not allowing children to read or learn things on their own if they are deemed &quot;too young for that&quot;.&quot; I think that there might be an exception for sexual topics, though. Precocious sexuality can be a sign of sexual abuse, and allowing it to continue might well be abusive in its own right."},{"owner":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605671880,"post_id":40789,"comment_id":85613,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;Your child is a peer, from day 1.&quot; There&#39;s strong academic research into neurological development that suggests that this isn&#39;t true. Your brain hasn&#39;t finished developing until well into your twenties; even teenagers aren&#39;t peers to adults yet, regardless of their exclamations to the contrary, since the part of the brain responsible for rational decision making has yet to come fully online."},{"owner":{"account_id":160089,"reputation":2002,"user_id":9837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7ee099fe1b5382177ba5645dbb1d50bc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"R.. GitHub STOP HELPING ICE","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9837/r-github-stop-helping-ice"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605674363,"post_id":40779,"comment_id":85614,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The part about IQ is completely wrong. IQ is a eugenecist invention for laundering privilege (mainly, common cultural context with the people designing the assessment) into claims of innate superiority. It is not an immutable characteristic of a child because it&#39;s not even a characteristic of the child."},{"owner":{"account_id":1554324,"reputation":357,"user_id":27621,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a7c19e1d6860e0e908fe3bdd165f3eb9?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"RonJohn","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27621/ronjohn"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":160089,"reputation":2002,"user_id":9837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7ee099fe1b5382177ba5645dbb1d50bc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"R.. GitHub STOP HELPING ICE","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9837/r-github-stop-helping-ice"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1605676281,"post_id":40779,"comment_id":85615,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@R..GitHubSTOPHELPINGICE if that&#39;s the case, then it spectacularly failed to show that members of the &quot;Yellow Peril&quot; are inferior."},{"owner":{"account_id":3795627,"reputation":2433,"user_id":7440,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/ulVTa.png?s=256","display_name":"Peter - Reinstate Monica","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7440/peter-reinstate-monica"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605686895,"post_id":40789,"comment_id":85619,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@nick012000 That children&#39;s (and, to a lesser degree, teens&#39;) brains aren&#39;t fully developed seems obvious. That does not mean they cannot be &quot;peers&quot;. What I mean with that is: Take them seriously. They are not &quot;inferior&quot;. They participate in family decision making (even if adults may have the final word because their wish is not doable). Let them feel the consequences of their actions and wishes, protect and guide them where necessary. Maybe the key word is <i>respect</i> here. Don&#39;t be manipulative. Tell them the truth (always). Adults aren&#39;t fully responsible and rational beings either! (ctd.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":3795627,"reputation":2433,"user_id":7440,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/ulVTa.png?s=256","display_name":"Peter - Reinstate Monica","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7440/peter-reinstate-monica"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605687065,"post_id":40789,"comment_id":85620,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"(ctd.:) So in essence the attitude is similar to the one you may have with a close adult friend. Plus, of course, the unconditional &quot;I&#39;ll be there for you no matter what&quot; which comes as part of a parent-child relationship. I think that&#39;s the best way to let them develop their own footing."},{"owner":{"account_id":4724252,"reputation":179,"user_id":37569,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/JAd8n.png?s=256","display_name":"JRE","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/37569/jre"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605693914,"post_id":40782,"comment_id":85623,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@countiblis:  How many normal parents do you expect are able to pay for tutors for multiple foreign languages?  You cannot do algebra if you cannot do simple math."},{"owner":{"account_id":522706,"reputation":279,"user_id":18949,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/bffff1c42112f47e7cbcde48a6e55e92?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"JVC","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/18949/jvc"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605703955,"post_id":40778,"comment_id":85625,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Well yes absolutely, in fact the current trend of sexual <i>instruction</i> in many schools (as opposed to basic <i>education</i>) also horrifies me.  All just makes me that much happier to have opted out of human children!"},{"owner":{"account_id":8447955,"reputation":103,"user_id":27471,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/ef4feaacbcbd03edc4fb721854ad3f75?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Rodrigo de Azevedo","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27471/rodrigo-de-azevedo"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":232345,"reputation":126,"user_id":14821,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5aedd802dca0d3b7397664dfac645ae8?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"anonymous","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14821/anonymous"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605704006,"post_id":40785,"comment_id":85626,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anonymous The Polg&#225;r sisters chose their ancestors wisely."},{"owner":{"account_id":232345,"reputation":126,"user_id":14821,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5aedd802dca0d3b7397664dfac645ae8?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"anonymous","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14821/anonymous"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":8447955,"reputation":103,"user_id":27471,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/ef4feaacbcbd03edc4fb721854ad3f75?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Rodrigo de Azevedo","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27471/rodrigo-de-azevedo"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605705618,"post_id":40785,"comment_id":85627,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@RodrigodeAzevedo I agree. Surely there is selection bias. But in any case that experiment shows that this educational strategy cannot be dismissed with a simple &quot;it won&#39;t work, and will only produce frustrated individuals&quot;."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1605712190,"post_id":40775,"comment_id":85631,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anongoodnurse Fair enough, your comment read that way to me with the following couple of sentences, though.  And yes, I do think that any talking is better than playing a recording - that&#39;s my understanding of the literature, though I suspect it&#39;s very nuanced in general."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605726398,"post_id":40779,"comment_id":85636,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@RonJohn  - Your answer isn&#39;t being picked on. It simply fails to meet the site&#39;s standards. When stating opinion as hard facts, we ask that sources be provided. You received two (upvoted) comments asking for sources, yet won&#39;t provide any. Not every answer requires citations, because not every answer presents a controversial issue as fact. I hope this explanation helps. If you want the flag removed, it&#39;s simple: provide reasonable sources for your information. If you do so, I&#39;ll remove my comment and undo my downvote."},{"owner":{"account_id":484995,"reputation":239,"user_id":32787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/JpOuqhG2.png?s=256","display_name":"MilkLife","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32787/milklife"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605727441,"post_id":40801,"comment_id":85637,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;I guess that they did not do anything special.&quot; =&gt; I&#39;m not sure if that&#39;s a valid assumption to make. I know all kinds of high IQ people and most had highly involved parents or other relatives. You can of course rely on luck, but if you want your child to be better than others, you&#39;ll probably need to be an above average parent too."},{"owner":{"account_id":8447955,"reputation":103,"user_id":27471,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/ef4feaacbcbd03edc4fb721854ad3f75?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Rodrigo de Azevedo","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27471/rodrigo-de-azevedo"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605727873,"post_id":40777,"comment_id":85638,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Graham Why would you take Gould seriously? He has a horrible reputation for a reason."},{"owner":{"account_id":4040106,"reputation":101,"user_id":39963,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/7PNXP.png?s=256","display_name":"somebody","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39963/somebody"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605752019,"post_id":40770,"comment_id":85640,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Okay, sure, so screens are bad, but I believe it isn&#39;t quite a full answer - there are alternative to screens, yes? Maybe books wouldn&#39;t work, but velcro boards (if those are still popular) and/or posters may certainly be an option"},{"owner":{"account_id":2222583,"reputation":9991,"user_id":6061,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":59,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/tNrYV.png?s=256","display_name":"Dariusz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6061/dariusz"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":4040106,"reputation":101,"user_id":39963,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/7PNXP.png?s=256","display_name":"somebody","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39963/somebody"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605766892,"post_id":40770,"comment_id":85642,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Question is &quot;should I show [...] videos&quot;. There&#39;s another question that covers the topic you&#39;re mentioning: <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/40771/what-can-i-do-to-a-6-month-old-child-so-she-ends-up-smart-and-has-a-high-iq\" title=\"what can i do to a 6 month old child so she ends up smart and has a high iq\">parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/40771/&hellip;</a>."},{"owner":{"account_id":1371621,"reputation":221,"user_id":36353,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/93e8075054832e9dbaa4f1f5fb326a53?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Will","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36353/will"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":4040106,"reputation":101,"user_id":39963,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/7PNXP.png?s=256","display_name":"somebody","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39963/somebody"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605787556,"post_id":40770,"comment_id":85649,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@somebody there aren&#39;t alternatives to screens that teach six-month-olds arithmetic. Any stimulus that an infant that age even superficially responds to is going to be excessive if you can meaningfully describe it as conveying principles of arithmetic. Of course something that means something about arithmetic to an older child or adult might have the same developmental value to the child as a nonsense poem or a recipe or a PhD thesis but then the mathematical content is rather beside the point."},{"owner":{"account_id":5706210,"reputation":101,"user_id":32641,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f6f574617919d7bcf84b9c30df85f28d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"DreamConspiracy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32641/dreamconspiracy"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605789822,"post_id":40789,"comment_id":85650,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@nick012000 there&#39;s also strong academic research indicating that having a lower IQ negatively correlates with rational decision making, memory, learning, and some similar concepts. Does that mean people with a lower IQ than yours aren&#39;t your peers either?"},{"owner":{"account_id":5706210,"reputation":101,"user_id":32641,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f6f574617919d7bcf84b9c30df85f28d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"DreamConspiracy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32641/dreamconspiracy"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1605790551,"post_id":40800,"comment_id":85651,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The multiple intelligences theory that you mention is generally rejected by mainstream psychology since the statistical evidence for it is lacking. See <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theory_of_multiple_intelligences#Lack_of_empirical_evidence\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this section in Wikiepdia</a> and especially the associated citations which seem excellent for the full details. Of course that does not mean that the behavior suggested in this answer is a bad idea- just that there being multiple intelligences isn&#39;t a good reason for it."},{"owner":{"account_id":4327858,"reputation":935,"user_id":9039,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/8F9Km.gif?s=256","display_name":"barbecue","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9039/barbecue"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":160089,"reputation":2002,"user_id":9837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7ee099fe1b5382177ba5645dbb1d50bc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"R.. GitHub STOP HELPING ICE","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9837/r-github-stop-helping-ice"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605806051,"post_id":40788,"comment_id":85652,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@R..GitHubSTOPHELPINGICE your comment seems irrelevant. Neither the OP nor this answer ever mention 18 months. The question was about a SIX month old."},{"owner":{"account_id":6655813,"reputation":107,"user_id":32337,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fbfvN.jpg?s=256","display_name":"&#198;zor &#198;hai -him-","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32337/%c3%86zor-%c3%86hai-him"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605808161,"post_id":40772,"comment_id":85654,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Your last link is directing me to some rad.stackexchange link and doesn&#39;t work."},{"owner":{"account_id":6655813,"reputation":107,"user_id":32337,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fbfvN.jpg?s=256","display_name":"&#198;zor &#198;hai -him-","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32337/%c3%86zor-%c3%86hai-him"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605808251,"post_id":40772,"comment_id":85655,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I have a background in developmental science and linguistics, and I&#39;m not really convinced that &lt;18 mos really pay meaningful attention to video chatting either. I think that should be read as &quot;It&#39;s okay to let Nana say hi to baby,&quot; not &quot;baby will get a lot of out of video chatting Nana&quot;"},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":6655813,"reputation":107,"user_id":32337,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fbfvN.jpg?s=256","display_name":"&#198;zor &#198;hai -him-","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32337/%c3%86zor-%c3%86hai-him"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605812696,"post_id":40772,"comment_id":85656,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@AzorAhai-him- : I double-checked and it works. Copy and paste it into the browser if necessary. Here it is again, just in case: <a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/0983263302\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://www.amazon.com/Brain-Rules-Baby-Raise-Smart/dp/09832&zwnj;&#8203;63302/</a> or try the book web site: <a href=\"http://www.brainrules.net/brain-rules-for-baby\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://www.brainrules.net/brain-rules-for-baby</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":6655813,"reputation":107,"user_id":32337,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fbfvN.jpg?s=256","display_name":"&#198;zor &#198;hai -him-","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32337/%c3%86zor-%c3%86hai-him"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605814441,"post_id":40772,"comment_id":85657,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@TimurShtatland Oh, SE seems to be capturing the amazon link and my ad-blocking stopped it. Thanks for the link to the actual book page."},{"owner":{"account_id":6655813,"reputation":107,"user_id":32337,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fbfvN.jpg?s=256","display_name":"&#198;zor &#198;hai -him-","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32337/%c3%86zor-%c3%86hai-him"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":160089,"reputation":2002,"user_id":9837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7ee099fe1b5382177ba5645dbb1d50bc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"R.. GitHub STOP HELPING ICE","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9837/r-github-stop-helping-ice"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605815010,"post_id":40774,"comment_id":85658,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@R.. The problem is relying on screens as a substitute for <i>in person social stimulation</i> which absolutely doesn&#39;t work. As of yet, no problems have emerged with parents relying on screens to teach their children to walk. So Joe&#39;s statement is true for the question at hand, if a little imprecise. In any case, kids waving back at the person on the screen is not the same as waving at a person who can wave back, or crawling to a toy, and turning it over in their hand, and bringing it to Dad (etc)."},{"owner":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":5706210,"reputation":101,"user_id":32641,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f6f574617919d7bcf84b9c30df85f28d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"DreamConspiracy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32641/dreamconspiracy"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605817103,"post_id":40789,"comment_id":85659,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@DreamConspiracy Generally, yes, considering that my personal peers would be other postgraduate students. More to the point, though, is that children aren’t little adults, and treating them that way can be harmful to their development. Treating them like they were was an educational fad that didn’t work."},{"owner":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":232345,"reputation":126,"user_id":14821,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5aedd802dca0d3b7397664dfac645ae8?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"anonymous","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14821/anonymous"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605817474,"post_id":40785,"comment_id":85661,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anonymous Like becoming an Olympic athlete, I suspect that becoming a chess grandmaster requires both genetics and lots of hard work from an early age. Without either you’re doomed to mediocrity."},{"owner":{"account_id":71901,"reputation":101,"user_id":3318,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c9ffc6f77d7f8ee002acd0e9ebc872f2?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Victor Sergienko","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/3318/victor-sergienko"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605817981,"post_id":40790,"comment_id":85662,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The paper is not about &quot;too much focus on intelligence&quot;, it&#39;s only about &quot;too much praise for intelligence&quot;. Which is obviously not the only way to focus on intelligence."},{"owner":{"account_id":71901,"reputation":101,"user_id":3318,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c9ffc6f77d7f8ee002acd0e9ebc872f2?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Victor Sergienko","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/3318/victor-sergienko"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605818428,"post_id":40770,"comment_id":85663,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Out of 4 quoted sources, only 4th (the WHO article) is scientifically credible, and it has nothing to do with education; it is &quot;WHO guidelines on physical activity, sedentary behaviour and sleep&quot;."},{"owner":{"account_id":3795627,"reputation":2433,"user_id":7440,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/ulVTa.png?s=256","display_name":"Peter - Reinstate Monica","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7440/peter-reinstate-monica"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605863423,"post_id":40789,"comment_id":85688,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@nick012000 Well, &quot;treating them as adults&quot; indeed seems wrong. &quot;Peers&quot; is perhaps a bad word choice -- &quot;not condescending&quot;, &quot;not manipulative&quot;, &quot;respect&quot;... perhaps I&#39;ll try to reword it. My central argument is to have meaningful, honest  conversations."},{"owner":{"account_id":5706210,"reputation":101,"user_id":32641,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f6f574617919d7bcf84b9c30df85f28d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"DreamConspiracy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32641/dreamconspiracy"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605871869,"post_id":40789,"comment_id":85691,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@nick012000 that&#39;s not the question that was asked. Your statement indicated that you thought you <i>could not</i> think of teenagers as peers due to their inferior brains. I asked if the same thing applied to people with a lower IQ than yours. A response focusing on circumstances in your personal life doesn&#39;t answer the question, so let me maybe phrase it more accurately: Do you think that people with an IQ lower than yours <i>could not be your peers either</i>?"},{"owner":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":5706210,"reputation":101,"user_id":32641,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f6f574617919d7bcf84b9c30df85f28d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"DreamConspiracy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32641/dreamconspiracy"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605918612,"post_id":40789,"comment_id":85723,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@DreamConspiracy Not without a lot of work to overcome their handicap, no. Like I said, my peers are other postgraduate students."},{"owner":{"account_id":103001,"reputation":101,"user_id":39987,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c877a65a343d0ad03a2d4c2b82029c46?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Mashmagar","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39987/mashmagar"},"edited":false,"score":18,"creation_date":1606147612,"post_id":40816,"comment_id":85753,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I don&#39;t see how this design would involve the wood touching the back of the fridge or even significantly impede airflow. Contacting the manufacturer seems like undue caution."},{"owner":{"account_id":484995,"reputation":239,"user_id":32787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/JpOuqhG2.png?s=256","display_name":"MilkLife","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32787/milklife"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1606147951,"post_id":40817,"comment_id":85754,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It would be useful to reference the data Pinker uses in his book to justify his claims. Likewise for Gopnik. Presumably it should be in scientific journals."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":7,"creation_date":1606154576,"post_id":40818,"comment_id":85756,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I would not suggest using anything that was potentially flammable in such a situation; while it should not get that hot, if something goes wrong it could.  This seems more likely to cause a problem when the baby pushes it in and ends up overheating the fridge..."},{"owner":{"account_id":1968759,"reputation":3144,"user_id":13650,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/723ef9d059b1582d42f1f3bd85326cd3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Cdn_Dev","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13650/cdn-dev"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":484995,"reputation":239,"user_id":32787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/JpOuqhG2.png?s=256","display_name":"MilkLife","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32787/milklife"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1606155467,"post_id":40817,"comment_id":85757,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"This is why this particular section of Stack is such a mess and why I don&#39;t participate anymore - I don&#39;t have time to parse through Google Scholar to provide a phenomenal answer on the internet, and yet I get downvoted for providing a thoughtful, good answer that points out two professionals who have done considerable research on the subject."},{"owner":{"account_id":385436,"reputation":173,"user_id":27835,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c87fe41c4e81d60897d2fca7402d4f04?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"CynicallyNaive","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27835/cynicallynaive"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1606156609,"post_id":40817,"comment_id":85758,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Downvoting for that reason would be (or is) absurd. Even in academic writing it&#39;s common to cite a reference as a gateway to other more detailed references. Thank you for the effort put into a thoughtful answer."},{"owner":{"account_id":2638908,"reputation":101,"user_id":7960,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/eJ0IS.png?s=256","display_name":"NotThatGuy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7960/notthatguy"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1606174871,"post_id":40816,"comment_id":85768,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@TimurShtatland Companies are often overly cautious about helping with DIY sort of things (especially ones that may go against recommended practices) to avoid getting sued. They will likely just recommend <i>not</i> filling the gap, letting a professional handyman figure it out or that you buy their tailor-made gap-filler, on the off chance that they happen to sell one."},{"owner":{"account_id":2222583,"reputation":9991,"user_id":6061,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":59,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/tNrYV.png?s=256","display_name":"Dariusz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6061/dariusz"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":5706210,"reputation":101,"user_id":32641,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f6f574617919d7bcf84b9c30df85f28d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"DreamConspiracy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32641/dreamconspiracy"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1606212715,"post_id":40800,"comment_id":85772,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I edited the answer and removed the questionable image. The main idea behind my answer still stands: there&#39;s much more to growing up and living than being smart."},{"owner":{"account_id":1779824,"reputation":291,"user_id":26760,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/9e6c41dfd6d23ea6dc57bd68170dbca7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Tim","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26760/tim"},"edited":false,"score":8,"creation_date":1606223332,"post_id":40820,"comment_id":85775,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Love the last para..."},{"owner":{"account_id":2717324,"reputation":409,"user_id":33670,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/MnkGM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"FreeMan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33670/freeman"},"edited":false,"score":8,"creation_date":1606229605,"post_id":40816,"comment_id":85776,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"+1 for allowing supervised exploration! Especially if you guide him to hurting himself (slightly, I&#39;m not suggesting a hospital-visit style injury) he&#39;ll lose interest quickly. Kids are curious, but they&#39;re rarely masochistic. Also, a few strong magnets glued to a piece of plywood will likely hold the board to the side of the fridge well enough to keep a tyke from getting behind there once he&#39;s learned that there&#39;s nothing interesting/not painful to explore. Leave the wood a few inches off the floor to allow cool air in, then go up 2-3 feet - sufficient to keep him out yet allow air flow."},{"owner":{"account_id":5457015,"reputation":101,"user_id":32216,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/x1byc.jpg?s=256","display_name":"BruceWayne","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32216/brucewayne"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1606246239,"post_id":40816,"comment_id":85778,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Also, OP doesn&#39;t have to necessarily install a panel that goes all the way up to the top of the fridge. Just enough for a 10m-1 year old to not reach. And I&#39;d bet after a few times of finding a panel instead of a fun gap to stick his hands in, he&#39;ll move on and OP could remove the panel after a few days of lost interest."},{"owner":{"account_id":391098,"reputation":101,"user_id":28939,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/iux9g.png?s=256","display_name":"davidbak","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/28939/davidbak"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1606250896,"post_id":40818,"comment_id":85779,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"or pulls it out and stuffs it in the oven and leaves it there ..."},{"owner":{"account_id":5109620,"reputation":101,"user_id":39058,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh5.googleusercontent.com/-twCUNUcC7jc/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAABgw/-xFwOg7Mv_A/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"A C","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39058/a-c"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1796213,"reputation":3431,"user_id":21986,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/991a4a6ddb04ff0a0d0ca93849f9b0ac?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"learner101","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/21986/learner101"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1606285038,"post_id":40816,"comment_id":85785,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@learner101 just remember that many magnets strong enough to hold the board to the fridge will also be capable of &quot;snapping&quot; together with a great amount of force!"},{"owner":{"account_id":58219,"reputation":353,"user_id":6975,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/afc5b8351b0622aab55049c7a48a6fca?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Emilio M Bumachar","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6975/emilio-m-bumachar"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1606327823,"post_id":40816,"comment_id":85793,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"If you&#39;re going to let him touch it once, unplug it before just in case."},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1608442877,"post_id":40828,"comment_id":85942,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"While I understand the theory of crying it out, your post has one conclusion that seems the crux of it:  clean, fed and “there isn&#39;t anything that you see” -&gt; I learned the hard way that some issues can’t be easily seen, yet need addressing, not letting the baby cry."},{"owner":{"account_id":3595277,"reputation":170,"user_id":20978,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/BK1fo.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Carcigenicate","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/20978/carcigenicate"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1609974941,"post_id":40992,"comment_id":86156,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Why sign language? Is the gesture actually easier for toddlers to grasp than saying &quot;done&quot;?"},{"owner":{"account_id":11316206,"reputation":101,"user_id":29469,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/59ba84ab12ac5ec1f558a0f746eb8c0e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Austin Hemmelgarn","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/29469/austin-hemmelgarn"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3595277,"reputation":170,"user_id":20978,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/BK1fo.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Carcigenicate","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/20978/carcigenicate"},"edited":false,"score":8,"creation_date":1609983710,"post_id":40992,"comment_id":86157,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Carcigenicate It gives them something else to do with their hands instead of using them to throw food. For many people, ‘retraining’ a habit is much easier when you can’t do both the old habit and the desired new habit at the same time, and this largely applies to small children just as well as it does to adults (and animals for that matter, it’s seems to be kind of wired in at a subconscious level)."},{"owner":{"account_id":3401915,"reputation":453,"user_id":14699,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a66cede09ad91417502051a3a146210a?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"curiousdannii","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14699/curiousdannii"},"edited":false,"score":5,"creation_date":1609995517,"post_id":40992,"comment_id":86158,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"And yes, many children can learn simple signs before they can speak. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baby_sign_language\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Teaching infants signs is fairly common</a>."},{"owner":{"account_id":330962,"reputation":152,"user_id":16366,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c807195c3caef0cc89ed8bbf9717a710?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Brondahl","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16366/brondahl"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3595277,"reputation":170,"user_id":20978,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/BK1fo.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Carcigenicate","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/20978/carcigenicate"},"edited":false,"score":6,"creation_date":1610015663,"post_id":40992,"comment_id":86159,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Carcigenicate. Talking is an insanely complicated physiological task, requiring incredibly subtle manipulations on multiple different muscle groups in precise coordination. We just don&#39;t notice because we learnt so long ago."},{"owner":{"account_id":3939334,"reputation":281,"user_id":33620,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f911774749a6cad95d07c7f63a01bed3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Tanaya","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33620/tanaya"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1610049758,"post_id":40992,"comment_id":86168,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Baby sign language is also the cutest form of actual communication you&#39;ll ever have with your child (citation needed)."},{"owner":{"account_id":126393,"reputation":179,"user_id":29843,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/759b484f17d919bf40270b5abefe45a7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"amara","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/29843/amara"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":330962,"reputation":152,"user_id":16366,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c807195c3caef0cc89ed8bbf9717a710?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Brondahl","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16366/brondahl"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1610050558,"post_id":40992,"comment_id":86169,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Brondahl Most people get traumatized out of remembering the process of learning. Without that loss, skill in learning language is not lost over time. How many years has it been since you played making animal noises for hundreds of hours? Every kid knows there&#39;s something wrong with the adults, even if they can&#39;t talk about it."},{"owner":{"account_id":7813880,"reputation":7904,"user_id":30366,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f996f3ea5fc3e9daa68d0f98db62b6be?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Meg","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30366/meg"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3939334,"reputation":281,"user_id":33620,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f911774749a6cad95d07c7f63a01bed3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Tanaya","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33620/tanaya"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1610051723,"post_id":40992,"comment_id":86173,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Tanaya  I support your theory.  When I found out that my pre-verbal baby had figured out how to combine signs to make new words to communicate -that even kind of made sense- it was one of the cutest days of my life.  Calling your toy unicorn &quot;corn + horse&quot;?  Obviously my child is a creative genius!  (Kidding... mostly!)"},{"owner":{"account_id":8163816,"reputation":854,"user_id":35525,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/TGg8q.jpg?s=256","display_name":"forest","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/35525/forest"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":160089,"reputation":2002,"user_id":9837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7ee099fe1b5382177ba5645dbb1d50bc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"R.. GitHub STOP HELPING ICE","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9837/r-github-stop-helping-ice"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1610326711,"post_id":40779,"comment_id":86239,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@R..GitHubSTOPHELPINGICE That is incorrect. While it may have been invented for that purpose, the modern view of IQ is that it is more or less immutable and is correlated with success (although is not the only or even most important factor). The problem with IQ tests is that they measure only a subset of intelligence (the subset that we know how to test). But IQ tests are consistent and someone will not test 80 one day and 120 the next. It might very by 5 or so points, but other than that, it&#39;s generally fixed, even if you practice for it. This is the prominent view in cognitive neuroscience."},{"owner":{"account_id":20316533,"reputation":853,"user_id":40190,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Gj5plYtcDIQpzM4Ol69AqnbTAojkE1SQnzn7PbH9A=k-s256","display_name":"Isabella Leonarda","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40190/isabella-leonarda"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1610755813,"post_id":41035,"comment_id":86277,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"thank you so much for your response. I totally agree that dumbing down is not optimal and I would strive to avoid it. Thank you for reminding to keep a natural pace."},{"owner":{"account_id":3915121,"reputation":5265,"user_id":6677,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":20,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fOaFT.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Jax","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6677/jax"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1610760596,"post_id":41035,"comment_id":86278,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I was wondering what’s wrong with “baby talk” if it’s done “naturally”? By that I mean, however it is that people who speak Russian normally speak to their babies? I thought that baby talk was something all, or most, cultures do in one form or another and that it’s beneficial?"},{"owner":{"account_id":11316206,"reputation":101,"user_id":29469,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/59ba84ab12ac5ec1f558a0f746eb8c0e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Austin Hemmelgarn","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/29469/austin-hemmelgarn"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3915121,"reputation":5265,"user_id":6677,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":20,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fOaFT.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Jax","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6677/jax"},"edited":false,"score":7,"creation_date":1610813083,"post_id":41035,"comment_id":86281,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Jax It’s beneficial to over-exaggerate the sounds and keep the vocabulary simple early on, but if you keep doing it for an extended period of time it can be detrimental because it avoids complexity in communication <i>at the time the child is most able to adapt to it</i>. Building vocabulary early helps significantly improve their understanding of the language, and not just using ‘baby talk’ all the time helps improve their listening comprehension."},{"owner":{"account_id":24885,"reputation":229,"user_id":7956,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/RIZKi.png?s=256","display_name":"Peter Mortensen","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7956/peter-mortensen"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1610877365,"post_id":41036,"comment_id":86286,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Isn&#39;t the choice between a 10% reduced vocabulary and two languages?"},{"owner":{"account_id":10452409,"reputation":235,"user_id":40156,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/196557e98134006cd06dd4363b95cd99?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"eipi","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40156/eipi"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1610878811,"post_id":41035,"comment_id":86287,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;d add that baby talk is appropriate at certain times moreso than others as they get older. Eg my toddler is almost 3 and I still use baby talk with her if she gets hurt or I am comforting her. Otherwise in day to day i try to use proper vocabulary and talk to her as normal."},{"owner":{"account_id":10452409,"reputation":235,"user_id":40156,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/196557e98134006cd06dd4363b95cd99?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"eipi","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40156/eipi"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1610880297,"post_id":41036,"comment_id":86288,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Also i think it dramatically depends on the kids development.  My daughter is talking in full sentences Bilingually at 32mo. Her cousin is also Bilingual and the same age and his vocabulary is 50 words. I think the variation in vocabulary development from one child to another is much larger than the difference between one language and two in the same child. So &#39;reduced vocabulary&#39; due to  Bilingualism is very hard to quantify, because you don&#39;t know what their vocabulary would have been had they been raised monolingual."},{"owner":{"account_id":5240719,"reputation":149,"user_id":24740,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/8ae06ea53a316cc879b9100b3ff25bfc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Sebastian Lenartowicz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/24740/sebastian-lenartowicz"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1610914065,"post_id":41035,"comment_id":86290,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Your mileage may vary on &quot;will never have an accent&quot; - I grew up speaking 2 languages at home, and to this day have an odd accent in the second one that native speakers immediately pick up on, but can never actually place."},{"owner":{"account_id":1855797,"reputation":150,"user_id":37801,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/54903e0733d46e7c72501b5cc1e62e5b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"The Dude","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/37801/the-dude"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1611161106,"post_id":41060,"comment_id":86341,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The C1 test consists of performing reading, writing, speaking and listening -- the latter in front of an examiner. Here is an example: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hijT9CCnH9Y\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">youtube.com/watch?v=hijT9CCnH9Y</a> I am sure it has changed a bit nowadays with COVID and everything, but probably not by much."},{"owner":{"account_id":1855797,"reputation":150,"user_id":37801,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/54903e0733d46e7c72501b5cc1e62e5b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"The Dude","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/37801/the-dude"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1611163873,"post_id":41065,"comment_id":86347,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I could manage to have most of my conversations with my kid in German, but the question is -- given that we are Americans and not Germans, what is the point? My wife and I have German heritage, sure, but also Polish, Lithuanian, English, French, Italian and Swedish. We are the quintessential American muts."},{"owner":{"account_id":1855797,"reputation":150,"user_id":37801,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/54903e0733d46e7c72501b5cc1e62e5b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"The Dude","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/37801/the-dude"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1611163935,"post_id":41065,"comment_id":86348,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I would much rather have them be able to use the language well (speaking, writing, reading and listening), but understand that it is a foreign language and mastering it may bring opportunity in the future."}],"has_more":true,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":266}